#!RuleTest
#test PMDA-AD0696 expect=violation domain=ADLBC
#note "No structure= declaration; detection = PARAMCD/AVAL -> BDS, + SPDEVID -> MEDICAL DEVICE BASIC DATA STRUCTURE. Group (001,DEV01,GLUC,LAST) has $ablfl_y_count=2 (rows 1+2, both ABLFL=Y) -> failing; row 3 (blank ABLFL) is a member of the same failing group but is not itself a baseline record; group (001,DEV01,ALB,LAST) count 1 -> clean. The shipped rule carries Grouping.Variables [USUBJID, SPDEVID, PARAMCD, BASETYPE], byte-matching the CDISC-AD0696 twin (Fix #277, ruling M2-D10), so the loader derives Sensitivity GROUP and ONE violation is anchored at the group's first flagged row -> count 1 at row=1. Without that block Sensitivity derived RECORD and rows 1, 2 AND 3 all fired - two on non-duplicate records and one on a record that is not a baseline at all. Phase 5b (D58/D94b): the check is group-constant, so ALL THREE rows of the failing group are flagged and ABLFL renders as the distinct set over them - [Y, ] (rows 1+2 Y, row 3 blank) - instead of the anchor row's Y. D94b's Shape B for this rule (drop ABLFL from Output_Variables, keep $ablfl_y_count) is a still-open corpus edit; until it lands the set is the honest value."
#library standard=adamig version=1-3
#expectViolationCount 1
#expectViolationAt row=1 SPDEVID=DEV01 PARAMCD=GLUC BASETYPE=LAST ABLFL="[Y, ]"
dataset ADLBC
col STUDYID  type=Char
col USUBJID  type=Char
col SPDEVID  type=Char
col PARAMCD  type=Char
col BASETYPE type=Char
col ABLFL    type=Char
col AVAL     type=Num
---
S1 | 001 | DEV01 | GLUC | LAST | Y | 100
S1 | 001 | DEV01 | GLUC | LAST | Y | 105
S1 | 001 | DEV01 | GLUC | LAST |   | 102
S1 | 001 | DEV01 | ALB  | LAST | Y | 30
